<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">Satellites (NASA and others)</data>
      <data key="d6">UserConsumerSatellite</data>
      <data key="d7">Existing satellites from organizations like NASA. Deliver a series of modestly processed and corrected tiles every day, throughout the day.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">EC2 Spot</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Large cluster of Spot instances spun up as quickly as possible to post-process incoming imagery (further correction and conversion to formats more accessible downstream), then spun back down.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">S3</data>
      <data key="d6">S3</data>
      <data key="d7">Bulk imagery data in relatively large compressed chunks. Catalog spans back 30 years, mostly within 10 years; about 5 PB total, growing about 100 GB/day and increasing as data sources are added. Read with HTTP range requests to fetch only part of an object.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">RDS</data>
      <data key="d6">RDS</data>
      <data key="d7">Metadata store. Used to look up which source imagery corresponds to a given tile or area.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">AWS Fargate</data>
      <data key="d6">Fargate</data>
      <data key="d7">Cluster serving web map tiles. Each map load is sliced into about 16 tiles arriving as individual requests. Looks up source imagery in RDS, pulls partial objects from S3, stitches the pieces into a larger image, downsamples to the right resolution, applies color correction (RGB) or color mapping (intensity values such as greenness 0 to 1), and serves the tile back.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">ElastiCache (Redis)</data>
      <data key="d6">ElastiCache</data>
      <data key="d7">Redis used as a central cache for already generated tiles. Common tiles (e.g. the whole-US view shown to most users at app load) are computed once on the fly, stored, and read/written as needed.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6">
      <data key="d5">Users / Farmers (Web App)</data>
      <data key="d6">UserConsumerWeb</data>
      <data key="d7">Users log into the app and see a map of the United States with the most up-to-date imagery; they can select the layer and time frame. Farmers supply their custom field boundary (area of interest), which is not known ahead of time.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="7">
      <data key="d5">Lambda</data>
      <data key="d6">Lambda</data>
      <data key="d7">A series of Lambda functions computing a time series from the source data cube. Looks up imagery in RDS, reads it from S3, builds the tile of interest, clips it to the farmer's area of interest and computes a summary (e.g. mean greenness of all pixels in the field), repeated about 1000 times to produce the time series. On-the-fly processing across millions of possible combinations saves orders of magnitude of cost.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Satellite tiles arrive every day, throughout the day, and are processed by the EC2 Spot cluster.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Processed bulk imagery outputs are stored in S3 (about 100 GB/day).</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Metadata about the processed imagery is stored in RDS.</data>
      <data key="d10">1'</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Fargate pulls only the needed subsection of the large compressed objects from S3 using HTTP range requests.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">Lambda reads the source imagery from S3, clips it to the area of interest and computes the summary (e.g. mean greenness), repeated about 1000 times to build the time series.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Fargate looks up in RDS which source imagery corresponds to the requested tile.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">Lambda looks up the needed imagery in RDS metadata.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Newly computed tiles are written to ElastiCache so they are computed on the fly only once.</data>
      <data key="d10">4</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">The stitched, downsampled and color corrected/mapped tile is served back to the user.</data>
      <data key="d10">5</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Fargate reads already generated tiles from the Redis cache instead of recomputing them.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Loading the map issues about 16 individual web tile requests to Fargate for the selected layer and time frame.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">The farmer-defined field boundary (area of interest) is passed to the series of Lambda functions.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">Indigo Carbon: Serverless Satellite Imagery Processing for Regenerative Agriculture</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FfSNnH2bbNc</data>
    <data key="d2">Indigo Carbon ingests satellite imagery (about 5 PB catalog, growing 100 GB/day), preprocesses it on EC2 Spot, stores bulk data in S3 and metadata in RDS, then serves on-the-fly web map tiles through Fargate (cached in ElastiCache Redis) and computes on-the-fly field-level time series with Lambda to generate carbon credits.</data>
    <data key="d3">Serverless, Analytics, Geospatial, Agriculture, Storage</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
